  "reconstructibility": 1,
  "reason": "The records collectively suggest that casting or quenching can retain metastable multiphase HEA structures, that composition changes can generate Laves phases, and that phase decomposition in Co–Fe–Ni-containing alloys can lower saturation magnetization while increasing coercivity. R7 additionally supplies relevant finite-temperature magnetic physics, while several close alloy systems demonstrate coexistence of fcc and ordered or secondary phases. However, none discloses the specific FeCoNiTi fcc/C14 combination, the compositionally matched C14-dominant comparator, Ti-sublattice antiferromagnetic coupling, or the proposed orbital-moment/spin-canting-assisted domain-wall pinning responsible for about 667 Oe coercivity. Thus the literature points in the right direction, but selecting this intervention and mechanism would require a substantial new idea."
